WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.258
Injection prevention begins with trust boundaries.

00:00:02.258 --> 00:00:06.933
In a JavaScript application, untrusted data can arrive through request bodies, query strings,

00:00:06.933 --> 00:00:10.529
route parameters, headers, cookies, file uploads, JSON payloads, form fields,

00:00:10.529 --> 00:00:13.046
client-side state, environment variables, message queues, webhooks,

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:14.484
and third-party API responses.

00:00:14.484 --> 00:00:19.519
Some of these sources feel internal, but they can still be influenced by users,

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.677
integrations, deployment systems, or compromised dependencies.

00:00:21.677 --> 00:00:25.992
The first defensive task is to identify where data enters application logic.

00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:29.228
A route handler may receive parameters from the URL.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:32.824
A background worker may receive a message from a queue.

00:00:32.824 --> 00:00:36.421
A webhook handler may receive data from a partner system.

00:00:36.421 --> 00:00:41.455
A frontend component may receive state from the browser or from an API response.

00:00:41.455 --> 00:00:46.130
Each entry point should have a clear expectation for type, shape, length, format,

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:46.849
and meaning.

00:00:46.849 --> 00:00:51.164
The second task is to map where data goes before sensitive use.

00:00:51.164 --> 00:00:55.120
A value may be validated in one layer, transformed in another,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.997
stored in a database, later rendered into HTML,

00:00:57.997 --> 00:01:00.514
and finally passed into a downstream API.

00:01:00.514 --> 00:01:05.549
Controls that were appropriate at entry may not be sufficient for a later context.

00:01:05.549 --> 00:01:09.864
Data flow review helps teams see when a value crosses from one

00:01:09.864 --> 00:01:12.022
interpreter or trust level into another.

00:01:12.022 --> 00:01:14.899
JavaScript applications often blur server and client responsibilities.

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:16.697
Client-side validation improves user experience,

00:01:16.697 --> 00:01:21.731
but it does not protect the server because clients can be modified or bypassed.

00:01:21.731 --> 00:01:24.608
Server-side validation and safe server-side construction remain necessary.

00:01:24.608 --> 00:01:29.643
Similarly, data that is safe for a database is not automatically safe for HTML,

00:01:29.643 --> 00:01:32.879
JSON embedded in a page, or a command argument.

00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:37.914
A practical data flow map does not need to be perfect to be useful.

00:01:37.914 --> 00:01:41.150
List the entry points, transformations, sensitive sinks, and controls.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:46.185
Then review the places where assumptions are weak or where data moves between contexts.

00:01:46.185 --> 00:01:50.500
That map becomes the foundation for secure design, code review, and testing.
